1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,670
Welcome back to Equation Station SA!

2
00:00:02,840 --> 00:00:07,650
I'm Chris, and this is our second Algebra
lesson for Grade twelve Mathematics.

3
00:00:08,220 --> 00:00:12,610
In our first lesson, we solved quadratic
equations by factorising.

4
00:00:13,000 --> 00:00:17,430
We looked for two numbers that multiply to
one value and add to another.

5
00:00:17,720 --> 00:00:21,290
And when the numbers cooperate,
that method is brilliant.

6
00:00:21,880 --> 00:00:27,470
But look at this equation: x squared minus
four x minus three equals zero.

7
00:00:28,000 --> 00:00:32,460
Can you quickly find two whole numbers that
multiply to negative three and add

8
00:00:32,480 --> 00:00:35,090
to negative four? Not easily.

9
00:00:35,520 --> 00:00:37,990
Does that mean the equation
has no solutions?

10
00:00:38,520 --> 00:00:42,350
Absolutely not. It means we
need another tool.

11
00:00:42,820 --> 00:00:46,460
Today, I'll show you the quadratic
formula, where it comes from,

12
00:00:46,480 --> 00:00:50,940
and how to use it confidently in a real
National Senior Certificate question.

13
00:00:50,960 --> 00:00:56,280
Then we're going to do something even more
interesting: predict what kind of

14
00:00:56,300 --> 00:00:59,910
roots a quadratic has, just by
looking at one number.

15
00:01:00,120 --> 00:01:03,490
And yes, you'll actually see that
happening on a graph.

16
00:01:03,820 --> 00:01:08,020
By the end, I want you to understand the
method, not just remember where the

17
00:01:08,040 --> 00:01:09,590
buttons are on your calculator.

18
00:01:10,260 --> 00:01:14,400
Before using any formula, ask yourself what
kind of equation you're looking

19
00:01:14,420 --> 00:01:19,150
at. A quadratic has the highest power of
the unknown equal to two.

20
00:01:19,420 --> 00:01:23,660
The standard form is a times x
squared, plus b times x,

21
00:01:23,680 --> 00:01:29,720
plus c, equals zero. Notice that a cannot be
zero, otherwise the squared term

22
00:01:29,740 --> 00:01:32,410
disappears and it isn't
quadratic any more.

23
00:01:32,940 --> 00:01:37,150
The letters a, b and c stand for
numbers called coefficients.

24
00:01:37,540 --> 00:01:39,090
And their signs matter.

25
00:01:39,500 --> 00:01:43,460
A negative coefficient must stay negative
when you read it from the equation.

26
00:01:43,480 --> 00:01:48,620
Think of standard form as a labelled starting
line: the x squared term comes

27
00:01:48,640 --> 00:01:52,410
first, the x term comes next,
then the constant.

28
00:01:52,780 --> 00:01:55,870
If a term is missing, its
coefficient is zero.

29
00:01:56,260 --> 00:02:00,800
What matters is where each part belongs, not
how complicated the numbers look.

30
00:02:00,820 --> 00:02:06,110
For our example, x squared minus four x
minus three equals zero,

31
00:02:06,540 --> 00:02:11,650
a is positive one, b is negative four,
and c is negative three.

32
00:02:11,900 --> 00:02:15,490
Not four and three. Negative four
and negative three.

33
00:02:15,660 --> 00:02:19,320
I want you to make this a habit:
write the values of a,

34
00:02:19,340 --> 00:02:22,590
b and c clearly before you
start substituting.

35
00:02:22,840 --> 00:02:26,240
It's a small step that prevents a lot
of unnecessary errors.

36
00:02:26,260 --> 00:02:29,170
Now, here is our new tool.

37
00:02:29,400 --> 00:02:34,720
The quadratic formula says: x equals negative
b, plus or minus the square root

38
00:02:34,740 --> 00:02:39,110
of b squared minus four a c, all
divided by two a.

39
00:02:39,460 --> 00:02:44,430
Look closely. The whole expression in the
numerator is divided by two a.

40
00:02:44,640 --> 00:02:48,270
The plus or minus sign gives us two
possible calculations.

41
00:02:48,720 --> 00:02:53,900
Imagine it as a fork in the road: one path
uses plus and the other uses minus.

42
00:02:53,920 --> 00:02:58,510
They belong to the same equation, and
we must check both paths.

43
00:02:58,780 --> 00:03:02,510
And everything inside that square root
has a very important job.

44
00:03:02,760 --> 00:03:04,690
It's called the discriminant.

45
00:03:04,920 --> 00:03:06,630
We will come back to it in a moment.

46
00:03:06,960 --> 00:03:10,250
You do not need to use the formula
for every quadratic.

47
00:03:10,400 --> 00:03:14,190
If something factorises easily,
factorising can be quicker.

48
00:03:14,360 --> 00:03:18,800
But the formula gives us a systematic way to
solve any quadratic equation.

49
00:03:18,820 --> 00:03:21,910
Sometimes it produces two real roots.

50
00:03:22,260 --> 00:03:24,750
Sometimes there is one repeated real root.

51
00:03:24,880 --> 00:03:27,490
And sometimes there are no real roots.

52
00:03:27,660 --> 00:03:31,760
The discriminant will tell us which case we're
dealing with before we even finish

53
00:03:31,780 --> 00:03:34,670
solving. But where did this
formula come from?

54
00:03:34,780 --> 00:03:36,670
It did not just fall out of the sky.

55
00:03:36,920 --> 00:03:39,930
It comes from a method called
completing the square.

56
00:03:40,180 --> 00:03:45,240
Let's get the idea using the very same
equation: x squared minus four x minus

57
00:03:45,260 --> 00:03:49,370
three equals zero. Move negative
three to the other side.

58
00:03:49,620 --> 00:03:53,050
We get x squared minus four
x equals three.

59
00:03:53,200 --> 00:03:55,750
Half of negative four is negative two.

60
00:03:55,900 --> 00:03:58,810
Square negative two and you
get positive four.

61
00:03:58,980 --> 00:04:00,650
Add four to both sides.

62
00:04:00,960 --> 00:04:05,100
Now the left side is x squared
minus four x plus four,

63
00:04:05,120 --> 00:04:07,610
which is x minus two, all squared.

64
00:04:07,820 --> 00:04:11,340
So we have x minus two,
squared, equals seven.

65
00:04:11,360 --> 00:04:13,590
And here's the important part.

66
00:04:13,820 --> 00:04:18,400
If a square equals seven, the quantity inside
that square can be positive square

67
00:04:18,420 --> 00:04:21,370
root of seven or negative
square root of seven.

68
00:04:21,560 --> 00:04:25,890
So x minus two equals plus or minus
the square root of seven.

69
00:04:26,000 --> 00:04:31,470
Add two to both sides, and x equals two plus
or minus the square root of seven.

70
00:04:31,560 --> 00:04:33,140
That's our pair of solutions.

71
00:04:33,160 --> 00:04:37,420
Completing the square is the reasoning behind
the general quadratic formula.

72
00:04:37,440 --> 00:04:41,540
We won't spend the whole lesson
deriving it for every possible a,

73
00:04:41,560 --> 00:04:46,200
b and c, but now you know there's a real
mathematical reason for that plus or

74
00:04:46,220 --> 00:04:51,040
minus. Let's solve the equation again, this
time with the actual formula,

75
00:04:51,060 --> 00:04:53,370
in the way you'd write your
working in an exam.

76
00:04:53,740 --> 00:04:57,890
Start with x squared minus four x
minus three equals zero.

77
00:04:58,160 --> 00:05:03,560
Remember: a equals one, b equals negative
four, and c equals negative three.

78
00:05:03,580 --> 00:05:08,993
The formula is negative b plus or minus the
square root of b squared minus four

79
00:05:09,013 --> 00:05:11,830
a c, all divided by two a.

80
00:05:12,080 --> 00:05:17,570
First, negative b. Since b is negative
four, negative b is positive four.

81
00:05:17,720 --> 00:05:23,450
Next, b squared. Negative four, in
brackets, squared is positive sixteen.

82
00:05:23,680 --> 00:05:26,570
Then subtract four times a times c.

83
00:05:26,660 --> 00:05:31,440
That's minus four times one times negative
three, so we're subtracting negative

84
00:05:31,460 --> 00:05:34,810
twelve. Sixteen plus twelve
gives twenty eight.

85
00:05:34,980 --> 00:05:37,970
The discriminant is twenty eight,
which is positive.

86
00:05:38,300 --> 00:05:40,190
Put those values into the formula.

87
00:05:40,420 --> 00:05:44,250
x equals four plus or minus the
square root of twenty eight,

88
00:05:44,480 --> 00:05:48,030
all over two. We can simplify the
square root of twenty eight.

89
00:05:48,460 --> 00:05:53,120
Twenty eight is four times seven, so its
square root is two times the square

90
00:05:53,140 --> 00:05:57,740
root of seven. That gives four plus or
minus two square root of seven,

91
00:05:57,760 --> 00:06:03,840
over two. Divide each term by two, and the
exact answer is x equals two plus

92
00:06:03,860 --> 00:06:05,870
or minus square root of seven.

93
00:06:06,080 --> 00:06:08,910
Now let's separate the two
possibilities clearly.

94
00:06:09,220 --> 00:06:13,520
The first root is two plus square root of
seven, which is approximately four

95
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:18,050
point six five. The second root is two
minus square root of seven,

96
00:06:18,320 --> 00:06:20,710
approximately negative zero
point six five.

97
00:06:21,120 --> 00:06:23,770
The exact answers are written
with square roots.

98
00:06:23,940 --> 00:06:26,040
The decimal answers are approximations.

99
00:06:26,060 --> 00:06:30,470
If an exam asks for two decimal
places, write the decimals.

100
00:06:30,520 --> 00:06:34,890
If it asks for exact answers, leave
them in simplified surd form.

101
00:06:35,000 --> 00:06:37,690
Don't round too early, or you
can lose accuracy.

102
00:06:38,040 --> 00:06:40,950
I want you to see why these two
answers are meaningful.

103
00:06:41,240 --> 00:06:46,610
Imagine drawing y equals x squared minus four
x minus three on a set of axes.

104
00:06:46,900 --> 00:06:51,220
Because the coefficient of x squared is
positive, the parabola opens upward.

105
00:06:51,240 --> 00:06:55,020
The curve comes down, reaches its
minimum at the point two,

106
00:06:55,040 --> 00:06:57,270
negative seven, and rises again.

107
00:06:57,520 --> 00:07:00,630
Watch the two places where it
crosses the horizontal axis.

108
00:07:00,940 --> 00:07:04,710
On the left, x is approximately
negative zero point six five.

109
00:07:05,000 --> 00:07:08,270
On the right, x is approximately
four point six five.

110
00:07:08,600 --> 00:07:11,510
Those are exactly the roots we
found using the formula.

111
00:07:11,680 --> 00:07:15,230
The algebra and the graph are
telling one consistent story.

112
00:07:15,500 --> 00:07:16,590
Notice something else.

113
00:07:16,740 --> 00:07:20,580
The roots are not nice whole numbers, but
they are still perfectly valid.

114
00:07:20,600 --> 00:07:24,580
Mathematics doesn't require our
answers to be tidy integers.

115
00:07:24,600 --> 00:07:28,090
A good method has to work even when
the numbers are awkward.

116
00:07:28,460 --> 00:07:33,610
Now it's your turn. Try two x squared plus
three x minus five equals zero.

117
00:07:33,740 --> 00:07:37,040
Before calculating anything, tell
me the values of a,

118
00:07:37,060 --> 00:07:43,010
b and c. Then calculate b squared minus four a
c, and use the quadratic formula.

119
00:07:43,060 --> 00:07:45,110
Pause the video and try it on paper.

120
00:07:45,320 --> 00:07:47,190
Give yourself a genuine attempt.

121
00:07:47,280 --> 00:07:49,400
I'll walk through the answer with
you when you come back.

122
00:07:49,420 --> 00:07:55,910
Welcome back. Here, a is two, b is positive
three, and c is negative five.

123
00:07:56,040 --> 00:08:00,650
The discriminant is three squared minus four
times two times negative five.

124
00:08:00,720 --> 00:08:03,690
That's nine plus forty, which
equals forty nine.

125
00:08:03,960 --> 00:08:08,630
Our formula gives x equals negative three plus
or minus the square root of forty

126
00:08:08,650 --> 00:08:12,510
nine, all over four. Square root of
forty nine is seven.

127
00:08:12,540 --> 00:08:17,480
So the first answer is negative three plus
seven, divided by four: x equals

128
00:08:17,500 --> 00:08:24,100
one. The second is negative three minus seven,
divided by four: x equals negative

129
00:08:24,120 --> 00:08:29,700
two point five. Check the first answer in the
original equation: two times one

130
00:08:29,720 --> 00:08:33,010
squared, plus three times one, minus five.

131
00:08:33,220 --> 00:08:36,790
That's two plus three minus
five, which is zero.

132
00:08:37,040 --> 00:08:40,700
For negative two point five, two
times six point two five,

133
00:08:40,720 --> 00:08:44,990
minus seven point five, minus
five also equals zero.

134
00:08:45,220 --> 00:08:50,120
Both answers work. Notice that the quadratic
formula has given us rational roots

135
00:08:50,140 --> 00:08:54,690
this time. So the formula does not
automatically mean messy decimals.

136
00:08:54,880 --> 00:08:58,510
It's a reliable method, whether the
answers are simple or not.

137
00:08:58,740 --> 00:09:03,547
Now let's investigate the number inside the
square root: b squared minus four

138
00:09:03,567 --> 00:09:08,410
a c. We call it the discriminant, written
with the Greek letter delta.

139
00:09:09,060 --> 00:09:13,430
Without calculating x, it tells us what
kind of real roots to expect.

140
00:09:14,020 --> 00:09:15,300
Don't memorise a table yet.

141
00:09:15,320 --> 00:09:20,240
Picture an upward opening parabola whose
lowest point is below the horizontal

142
00:09:20,260 --> 00:09:25,090
axis. It crosses the axis twice,
giving two different roots.

143
00:09:25,680 --> 00:09:27,170
Lift the curve slowly.

144
00:09:27,480 --> 00:09:31,950
The crossings approach each other until it
just touches the axis at one point:

145
00:09:32,140 --> 00:09:36,150
a repeated root. Lift it higher and
there are no crossings,

146
00:09:36,280 --> 00:09:40,680
so no real roots. We can actually
watch all three cases happen.

147
00:09:40,700 --> 00:09:45,140
Our moving curve is y equals x
minus two, all squared,

148
00:09:45,160 --> 00:09:48,830
plus k. The value of k
controls its height.

149
00:09:49,340 --> 00:09:53,070
When k is negative four, it
crosses at zero and four.

150
00:09:53,340 --> 00:09:56,070
At k equals zero, it touches at two.

151
00:09:56,360 --> 00:10:00,190
At k equals positive three, it
stays above the axis.

152
00:10:00,680 --> 00:10:02,890
One curve, three root patterns.

153
00:10:03,100 --> 00:10:05,150
That's what nature of roots describes.

154
00:10:05,720 --> 00:10:07,500
Now connect that picture to the formula.

155
00:10:07,520 --> 00:10:12,410
If the discriminant is positive, we take the
square root of a positive number.

156
00:10:12,620 --> 00:10:17,400
That gives two different choices, plus and
minus, so there are two distinct

157
00:10:17,420 --> 00:10:21,870
real roots. Graphically, the parabola
crosses the x axis twice.

158
00:10:22,500 --> 00:10:26,050
If the discriminant equals zero,
its square root is zero.

159
00:10:26,340 --> 00:10:29,650
Adding zero or subtracting zero
gives the same answer.

160
00:10:29,760 --> 00:10:34,360
We have real and equal roots, and the
parabola just touches the axis once.

161
00:10:34,380 --> 00:10:39,510
If the discriminant is negative, there is no
real square root of that number.

162
00:10:39,640 --> 00:10:44,110
That means no real solutions, and the
graph does not cross the x axis.

163
00:10:44,460 --> 00:10:49,270
That's the connection: positive delta,
two crossings; zero delta,

164
00:10:49,500 --> 00:10:53,490
one touching point; negative
delta, no real crossings.

165
00:10:53,760 --> 00:10:55,770
The formula and graph agree.

166
00:10:56,140 --> 00:11:00,560
Here's a very typical exam instruction:
determine the nature of the roots of

167
00:11:00,580 --> 00:11:03,600
x squared plus x plus one equals zero.

168
00:11:03,620 --> 00:11:06,070
Listen to what the question asks.

169
00:11:06,280 --> 00:11:08,330
It doesn't say solve for x.

170
00:11:08,500 --> 00:11:10,810
It asks for the nature of the roots.

171
00:11:11,120 --> 00:11:13,050
So we only need the discriminant.

172
00:11:13,640 --> 00:11:17,290
a equals one, b equals one,
and c equals one.

173
00:11:17,580 --> 00:11:21,650
Delta equals one squared minus four
times one times one.

174
00:11:21,680 --> 00:11:24,730
That's one minus four, which
gives negative three.

175
00:11:25,120 --> 00:11:28,690
Because delta is less than zero,
there are no real roots.

176
00:11:28,960 --> 00:11:33,140
We can stop there. There's no reason to
substitute into the entire quadratic

177
00:11:33,160 --> 00:11:36,750
formula when the discriminant
already answers the question.

178
00:11:37,180 --> 00:11:42,360
Graphically, y equals x squared plus x plus
one has a minimum at negative one

179
00:11:42,380 --> 00:11:44,330
half, positive three quarters.

180
00:11:44,560 --> 00:11:47,110
Even its lowest point is above the axis.

181
00:11:47,300 --> 00:11:48,650
There are no crossings.

182
00:11:48,940 --> 00:11:51,850
But the discriminant can tell us
something more precise.

183
00:11:52,240 --> 00:11:56,860
When it is positive, the two real roots
may be rational or irrational.

184
00:11:56,880 --> 00:12:00,890
Rational numbers can be written as
fractions of integers.

185
00:12:01,360 --> 00:12:04,550
Irrational numbers, like square
root of seven, cannot.

186
00:12:05,160 --> 00:12:10,040
For a quadratic with integer coefficients, a
positive perfect square discriminant

187
00:12:10,060 --> 00:12:14,460
gives rational roots, while a positive whole
number discriminant that is not

188
00:12:14,480 --> 00:12:17,270
a perfect square gives irrational roots.

189
00:12:17,660 --> 00:12:20,310
Compare the two equations we
have already solved.

190
00:12:20,600 --> 00:12:24,570
For two x squared plus three x
minus five equals zero,

191
00:12:24,880 --> 00:12:26,060
delta was forty nine.

192
00:12:26,080 --> 00:12:31,600
Forty nine is seven squared, and the roots
came out as one and negative two

193
00:12:31,620 --> 00:12:33,790
point five. Rational numbers.

194
00:12:34,500 --> 00:12:38,130
For x squared minus four x minus
three equals zero,

195
00:12:38,420 --> 00:12:39,870
delta was twenty eight.

196
00:12:40,380 --> 00:12:42,750
Twenty eight is not a perfect square.

197
00:12:42,940 --> 00:12:47,250
The exact roots contained square root of
seven, so they were irrational.

198
00:12:47,840 --> 00:12:52,640
Both equations had two distinct real roots;
their difference was the type of

199
00:12:52,660 --> 00:12:53,620
number in the answers.

200
00:12:53,640 --> 00:12:59,170
So, if a question asks for real,
rational and unequal roots,

201
00:12:59,280 --> 00:13:02,110
look for a positive perfect
square discriminant.

202
00:13:02,380 --> 00:13:07,740
If it asks for real, irrational and unequal
roots, look for a positive discriminant

203
00:13:07,760 --> 00:13:09,710
that is not a perfect square.

204
00:13:09,860 --> 00:13:14,400
The sign tells you the number of real roots;
the square root structure tells

205
00:13:14,420 --> 00:13:16,430
you the type. Quick check.

206
00:13:16,860 --> 00:13:20,270
If the discriminant is zero, what can
you say about the roots?

207
00:13:20,900 --> 00:13:26,810
Pause. They're real and equal: plus or
minus zero gives the same x value,

208
00:13:26,900 --> 00:13:29,230
and the graph just touches the axis.

209
00:13:29,820 --> 00:13:31,870
Here's a short Grade twelve challenge.

210
00:13:32,160 --> 00:13:37,960
For which values of k does x squared minus
four x plus k equals zero have real

211
00:13:37,980 --> 00:13:42,510
roots? We need a condition on k,
not actual x values.

212
00:13:42,820 --> 00:13:47,340
We know that real roots require a
discriminant greater than or equal to zero.

213
00:13:47,360 --> 00:13:51,870
That includes two distinct real
roots and equal real roots.

214
00:13:52,240 --> 00:13:56,750
Here a is one, b is negative
four, and c is k.

215
00:13:57,000 --> 00:14:01,950
So delta equals negative four squared
minus four times one times k.

216
00:14:02,140 --> 00:14:04,310
That's sixteen minus four k.

217
00:14:04,700 --> 00:14:08,510
Set sixteen minus four k greater
than or equal to zero.

218
00:14:08,980 --> 00:14:12,530
Rearranging gives four k less than
or equal to sixteen,

219
00:14:12,620 --> 00:14:15,540
and therefore k less than or
equal to four.

220
00:14:15,560 --> 00:14:20,460
Visualise it: the graph is y equals
x minus two squared,

221
00:14:20,480 --> 00:14:25,330
plus k minus four. At k equals
four, it touches the axis.

222
00:14:25,860 --> 00:14:27,770
Below four, it crosses twice.

223
00:14:28,280 --> 00:14:30,550
Above four, it misses entirely.

224
00:14:30,980 --> 00:14:33,110
The algebra and graph match.

225
00:14:33,620 --> 00:14:37,590
Now let's bring this into a real National
Senior Certificate examination.

226
00:14:38,020 --> 00:14:42,420
In Episode One, we looked at Question one
point one point one from the November

227
00:14:42,440 --> 00:14:45,040
twenty twenty five Mathematics Paper One.

228
00:14:45,060 --> 00:14:47,850
That question gave us factors to solve.

229
00:14:48,240 --> 00:14:53,030
The very next question, one point one
point two, tests today's method.

230
00:14:53,360 --> 00:14:54,610
It's worth four marks.

231
00:14:54,960 --> 00:15:00,190
Here it is: five x squared plus two
equals negative nine x.

232
00:15:00,660 --> 00:15:03,510
Solve for x, correct to two
decimal places.

233
00:15:03,960 --> 00:15:06,490
Before I do anything, you do something.

234
00:15:06,840 --> 00:15:07,990
Look at the equation.

235
00:15:08,500 --> 00:15:11,860
What must happen before we
identify a, b and c?

236
00:15:11,880 --> 00:15:16,890
You should move every term to the same side
so that the other side is zero.

237
00:15:17,540 --> 00:15:19,890
Pause for a moment and write
the standard form.

238
00:15:20,240 --> 00:15:23,490
Then decide whether the quadratic
formula is appropriate.

239
00:15:23,880 --> 00:15:28,040
This is a real four mark question, so imagine
you're writing the solution in

240
00:15:28,060 --> 00:15:29,890
your examination answer book.

241
00:15:30,120 --> 00:15:32,950
Ready? Add nine x to both sides.

242
00:15:33,240 --> 00:15:37,870
We get five x squared plus nine x
plus two equals zero.

243
00:15:38,100 --> 00:15:39,500
That's our standard form.

244
00:15:39,520 --> 00:15:43,370
We've now earned the first method mark
in the official guideline.

245
00:15:43,780 --> 00:15:49,360
Read the coefficients carefully: a equals
five, b equals positive nine,

246
00:15:49,380 --> 00:15:51,670
and c equals positive two.

247
00:15:51,940 --> 00:15:54,110
Now substitute into the formula.

248
00:15:54,540 --> 00:15:59,400
x equals negative nine, plus or minus the
square root of nine squared minus

249
00:15:59,420 --> 00:16:04,060
four times five times two, all
divided by two times five.

250
00:16:04,080 --> 00:16:09,530
Keep the entire numerator together and the
denominator as two times five.

251
00:16:09,740 --> 00:16:13,720
The official marking guideline awards a mark
for correctly substituting into

252
00:16:13,740 --> 00:16:17,720
the formula. Notice that the
substitution is visible on the page,

253
00:16:17,740 --> 00:16:20,170
so the examiner can follow your reasoning.

254
00:16:20,540 --> 00:16:24,610
Calculate inside the square root:
nine squared is eighty one.

255
00:16:25,000 --> 00:16:27,830
Four times five times two is forty.

256
00:16:28,320 --> 00:16:30,440
Eighty one minus forty is forty one.

257
00:16:30,460 --> 00:16:35,360
So x equals negative nine plus or minus
the square root of forty one,

258
00:16:35,380 --> 00:16:39,690
all over ten. Keep that exact
expression while you work.

259
00:16:39,820 --> 00:16:43,560
It's easier to avoid rounding errors when you
don't approximate the square root

260
00:16:43,580 --> 00:16:46,710
too early. Now we calculate both branches.

261
00:16:47,380 --> 00:16:50,560
First, negative nine plus the
square root of forty one,

262
00:16:50,580 --> 00:16:55,880
divided by ten. Rounded to two decimal
places, that gives negative zero point

263
00:16:55,900 --> 00:17:00,880
two six. Second, negative nine minus the
square root of forty one,

264
00:17:00,900 --> 00:17:06,167
divided by ten. Rounded to two decimal
places, that gives negative one point

265
00:17:06,187 --> 00:17:09,210
five four. Those are our two answers.

266
00:17:09,580 --> 00:17:12,070
One mark is awarded for each correct root.

267
00:17:12,300 --> 00:17:16,230
One mark for standard form, one for
correct formula substitution,

268
00:17:16,460 --> 00:17:18,230
and one for each answer.

269
00:17:18,440 --> 00:17:22,720
Four marks in total. Notice that the first
mark came before the formula.

270
00:17:22,740 --> 00:17:27,290
The examiner rewards organised working,
not just calculator answers.

271
00:17:28,020 --> 00:17:32,130
Rearrange, identify, substitute,
calculate both roots,

272
00:17:32,160 --> 00:17:34,390
and follow the requested answer format.

273
00:17:34,620 --> 00:17:38,310
The surd expression is exact; the
decimals are rounded.

274
00:17:38,380 --> 00:17:42,050
Let's finish our worked lesson with
one focused exam warning.

275
00:17:42,320 --> 00:17:44,310
Keep the same question on the screen.

276
00:17:44,600 --> 00:17:49,910
The value of b is positive nine, so
negative b is negative nine.

277
00:17:49,940 --> 00:17:51,800
Don't change that to positive nine.

278
00:17:51,820 --> 00:17:55,510
The plus or minus sign
represents two calculations,

279
00:17:55,620 --> 00:18:00,040
not one. And the denominator is two
a, which is ten here,

280
00:18:00,060 --> 00:18:02,050
not just a, which is five.

281
00:18:02,220 --> 00:18:06,510
Those three pieces belong to the same
formula and must stay together.

282
00:18:06,600 --> 00:18:10,950
Once your values are substituted carefully,
the rest is ordinary arithmetic.

283
00:18:11,260 --> 00:18:15,380
And when you check your calculator, make sure
you enter the entire numerator

284
00:18:15,400 --> 00:18:17,810
in brackets before dividing by ten.

285
00:18:18,080 --> 00:18:18,980
Three quick questions.

286
00:18:19,000 --> 00:18:22,970
If factorisation isn't convenient,
what's another method?

287
00:18:23,260 --> 00:18:24,530
The quadratic formula.

288
00:18:24,760 --> 00:18:27,490
If delta is negative, how many real roots?

289
00:18:27,900 --> 00:18:31,470
None. If a question asks only for
the nature of roots,

290
00:18:31,580 --> 00:18:33,010
must you fully solve it?

291
00:18:33,040 --> 00:18:35,510
No. The discriminant gives the answer.

292
00:18:35,620 --> 00:18:38,670
Here is the routine I want you to
remember for the examination.

293
00:18:39,000 --> 00:18:41,390
Put the quadratic into standard form.

294
00:18:41,460 --> 00:18:44,810
Read a, b and c, including their signs.

295
00:18:45,000 --> 00:18:48,720
Decide whether factorisation or the
formula is the smarter method.

296
00:18:48,740 --> 00:18:53,370
If you use the formula, calculate both
the plus and minus branches,

297
00:18:53,540 --> 00:18:56,170
then write your answers in the
requested form.

298
00:18:56,240 --> 00:18:59,150
If the question asks only about
the nature of roots,

299
00:18:59,420 --> 00:19:02,840
start with delta and stop when you have
answered what was asked.

300
00:19:02,860 --> 00:19:06,490
Now practise this independently on
Equation Station SA.

301
00:19:06,560 --> 00:19:08,590
Open our Algebra Mastery Bank.

302
00:19:08,920 --> 00:19:12,350
Question ten gives you a more challenging
quadratic formula problem.

303
00:19:12,540 --> 00:19:15,700
Question thirteen tests the value
of k and real roots.

304
00:19:15,720 --> 00:19:20,590
And Question seventeen connects the
discriminant with exact and decimal answers.

305
00:19:20,900 --> 00:19:23,750
Try the questions on paper before
revealing the memos.

306
00:19:23,940 --> 00:19:27,880
Then take the Algebra Test Your Knowledge quiz
to check which skills still need

307
00:19:27,900 --> 00:19:32,360
attention. In our next main video, we'll take
these same roots and use the shape

308
00:19:32,380 --> 00:19:35,050
of the parabola to solve
quadratic inequalities.

309
00:19:35,460 --> 00:19:38,390
Instead of asking only where the
graph crosses zero,

310
00:19:38,560 --> 00:19:41,750
we'll ask where it lies above
or below the axis.

311
00:19:41,960 --> 00:19:47,390
For now, remember this: a quadratic formula isn't
just a set of symbols to memorise.

312
00:19:47,600 --> 00:19:52,940
It connects equations, exact answers,
decimals, and graphs in one beautiful

313
00:19:52,960 --> 00:19:54,310
piece of mathematics.

314
00:19:54,700 --> 00:19:56,190
Thank you for learning with me.

315
00:19:56,320 --> 00:19:59,470
I'm Chris, and this is
Equation Station SA.

316
00:19:59,700 --> 00:20:00,720
See you in Lesson Three!
